      "thinking": "The evidence points to the monthly-spend-feature flag being toggled on at 17:47:37 by an admin, which triggered a ZeroDivisionError in spend_summary.py:47 \u2014 this is a latent code bug exposed by the flag flip, not a deployment issue, since code and config stayed unchanged. But I notice the onset timestamp was actually 17:42:00, earlier than the flag toggle, which seems worth reconciling.\n\nActually, looking closer, the logs show the flag already partially on at 17:42 as a mixed rollout, shifting to fully on by 17:43 \u2014 before the officially logged toggle at 17:47:37. This suggests either a gradual percentage rollout that wasn't captured in the change log, or a separate control mechanism for the flag. There's also a conflicting sequence of on/off toggles between admin and Argus around 17:47:37, 17:47:58, and 17:51:00 that I need to line up against Argus's own attempted fix.\n\nTurning the flag off apparently didn't stop the errors, since logs show \"on\" and errors persisting throughout 17:48-17:56 at a steady ~30-36% rate regardless of toggling \u2014 which suggests the flag itself may not be the root cause, even though the error spike at 17:42 lines up suspiciously with the \"on\" change at 17:47:37.\n\napparently didn't hold\u2014the flag was disabled but something re-enabled it, likely config drift or a self-healing mechanism, and the error kept recurring. This strongly supports a feature-flag-toggle causal chain: flag turned on, bad code path triggered, ZeroDivisionError results. Since it's this service's own bug, faulting_service should stay null, with subject monthly-spend-feature transitioning from off to on. Checking get_changes confirms no deployments occurred in this window, only flag toggles, so I'm confident this fully explains the alert.\n\nThe one puzzle is the timing: admin turned the flag on at 17:47:37, it was turned off at 17:47:58, then back on at 17:51:00\u2014yet the \"already tried\" note says the off toggle didn't fix things, which is odd since disabling it should have stopped the ZeroDivisionError immediately. The logs still showing \"on\" right after the toggle suggest the change might not have taken effect right away.\n\nI'm now wondering if this is a caching issue tied to io-summary-cache, which was restarted at 17:56 without resolving anything\u2014maybe the flag reads from a stale cached config. But io-summary-cache is described as optional and not essential to page correctness, so that seems like a weak lead. More likely, the ZeroDivisionError at spend_summary.py:47 comes from dividing by a count that's zero, possibly triggered by an empty cache rather than the flag toggle itself, though the error timing still lines up suspiciously well with the flag state changes.\n\nThe flag \"monthly-spend-feature\" gates the monthly spend summary code, which contains the division bug\u2014so when it's on, the buggy path executes. Since the change log shows the flag toggled off at 17:47:58 but the 17:48:00 rollup still shows \"on,\" this discrepancy is probably just propagation lag rather than the toggle failing outright.\n\nI'll settle on feature-flag-toggle as the primary hypothesis with confidence around 0.85: the incident began when the flag switched from off to on, exposing the ZeroDivisionError bug, and the fact that disabling it didn't seem to help suggests the off toggle failed to propagate \u2014 but that's a separate mitigation issue, not the root cause of the original alert.\n\n",
